      { "text": "Initiate empiric pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + leucovorin (folinic acid) for cerebral toxoplasmosis with re-imaging in 2-3 weeks; if no clinical/radiographic improvement, brain biopsy is performed to evaluate primary CNS lymphoma; trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is an alternative regimen; ART must also be initiated/optimized.", "explanation": "Correct. The lecturer specified that in AIDS patients with CD4 <100 and ring-enhancing brain lesions, the initial management is empiric anti-toxoplasmosis treatment because cerebral toxoplasmosis is the most common cause of focal CNS lesions in this population. Standard regimen is pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + leucovorin (folinic acid to prevent pyrimethamine-induced bone marrow suppression). Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is an alternative. Re-imaging is performed at 2-3 weeks: clinical and radiographic improvement supports the toxoplasmosis diagnosis, while non-response prompts brain biopsy to evaluate for primary CNS lymphoma (the major differential — the lecturer noted these can look identical on imaging). ART should also be initiated/optimized. Positive Toxoplasma IgG indicates prior exposure (most adults), supporting the empiric approach. CSF analysis can help differentiate toxoplasmosis from CNS lymphoma if available." },
      { "text": "Empiric ceftriaxone for bacterial meningitis without further imaging.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Ceftriaxone is for bacterial meningitis; the imaging shows ring-enhancing lesions, which are not characteristic of bacterial meningitis. This would be the correct answer if the patient had nuchal rigidity, fever, and findings consistent with bacterial meningitis (no focal lesions)." }
